Chapter: TlsRecordGuard
  cites Foreword chapter Tls

 Track D item 19, the Tls leg. tls-decode-record read five header bytes with
 five bare list-at calls and no length check at all, so any record shorter
 than its own header killed the guest before a single field was interpreted.

 Guarded because something runs it (red's ruling, extended to this leg
 2026-08-16): tls-test:36 and :41 decode through it. The chapter itself is
 cited in production by DtlsHello, DtlsMessage, TlsCert, TlsEndpoint and
 os/net/DtlsEndpoint, though none of those call this function today.

 The existing test is a round trip through tls-encode-record, so the decoder
 had only ever been shown records our own encoder wrote. A five-byte header
 is exactly what a truncated read off a socket does not have (L-GAP).

 tls-slice:205 was already bounded and clamps, so the PAYLOAD was never the
 hazard; the header was. The declared length is now reported rather than
 trusted: tls-rec-valid is False when the record is shorter than its header
 and when the length field claims more payload than arrived, which is the
 truncated-record case a socket produces and the round trip cannot.

 Row 6 is the discriminator. A record declaring zero payload bytes is
 LEGITIMATE and must stay valid, so a guard that refused every short record
 would show up there.

Section: Helpers

  show-bool : Boolean -> Text
  show-bool (b) = if b then "True" else "False"

  rec-row : List Integer, Text -> Text
  rec-row (bytes) (label) =
    let r = tls-decode-record bytes
    in label & " valid=" & show-bool (r.tls-rec-valid) & " type=" & show (r.tls-rec-type) & " plen=" & show (list-length (r.tls-rec-payload))

Section: Records

 A real handshake record from our own encoder, and a real application
 record: the positive controls.

  app-record : List Integer = tls-encode-record tls-content-application [72, 101, 108, 108, 111]

 One byte short of the five-byte header. Unguarded, list-at bytes 4 is out
 of range and the guest dies.

  four-bytes : List Integer = [23, 3, 3, 0]

 A header claiming 100 payload bytes with none behind it. tls-slice clamps
 to nothing, so the old decoder answered a confident record with an empty
 payload and no way for a caller to tell.

  truncated-payload : List Integer = [23, 3, 3, 0, 100]

 A legitimate zero-length record. Must stay valid.

  empty-payload : List Integer = [23, 3, 3, 0, 0]

Section: Body

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (rec-row app-record "1-app-record")
    print-line-uni (rec-row (tls-encode-record tls-content-handshake [1, 2, 3]) "2-handshake")
    print-line-uni (rec-row [] "3-empty")
    print-line-uni (rec-row four-bytes "4-four-bytes")
    print-line-uni (rec-row truncated-payload "5-truncated-payload")
    print-line-uni (rec-row empty-payload "6-empty-payload")
  end

